WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
Film Courage: What did you learn in film school&nbsp;
that you didn't already know? Evan Kidd: I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:11.440
film school certainly taught me like a lot of the&nbsp;
important basics of cinematography, of lighting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.880
making sure that microphones work in the&nbsp;
best way. But I do think it also taught me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:24.320
how to be resourceful because I think for for me&nbsp;
certainly something that happened was our film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.640
school didn't have a giant budget, didn't have&nbsp;
a giant production closet with all the gear we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.760
needed and everything like that. A lot of times&nbsp;
it would just be like here's the assignment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.840
make sure you find a way to make it happen and&nbsp;
that's just the way it went and I think a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.680
people might see that as a drawback but to&nbsp;
me like having those creative restrictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:52.320
kind of opened up the freedom a little bit and it&nbsp;
allowed me to sort of understand the way that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:58.640
would later be making a lot of films in the quote&nbsp;
unquote real world. I liked that I didn't ever get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.680
comfortable in the the film school bubble&nbsp;
because I know a lot of people that get in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.000
the film school bubble and then when it&nbsp;
pops after graduation they're like what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.280
the real world is crazy you know there's not&nbsp;
people wanting to hold my hand and you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:19.040
provide a full lighting kit and makeup department&nbsp;
you know it it it's it's fine like you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.960
that's one way to do it and you know i&nbsp;
truly think there's no you know right or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:28.560
wrong way but i just know for me that seemed&nbsp;
to resonate the most with what i was doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.040
so you appreciated having to be scrappy&nbsp;
and find things and and resourceful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:43.040
and not maybe not things being so easily&nbsp;
provided yeah yeah absolutely i think for me like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:49.840
the the scrappiness was almost the like&nbsp;
the fun of it you know it was almost like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.640
uh solving a puzzle and you know certainly like&nbsp;
there's a lot of headache that comes with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:01.520
scrappiness and it's not glamorous and i mean&nbsp;
you're not gonna sell people on filmmaking on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.120
scrappiness alone unless they've gone through it&nbsp;
um on paper you know if you're just describing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.440
to someone i think a lot of times people be like&nbsp;
why would you want to do that you know why would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:17.440
you put yourself through that um but i think when&nbsp;
you're surrounded with good people which is uh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.960
majorly majorly majorly important like making&nbsp;
sure the people on your crew the actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.360
um they're all in the same headspace with you&nbsp;
if you're in a project with them for you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.360
extended periods of time and you're you know&nbsp;
like staying for late hours and you know eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.320
like cheap meals around a campfire in the woods if&nbsp;
you're shooting on location like i mean you gotta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.840
make sure you like the people um but yeah i think&nbsp;
it's like when you finally get those circumstances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:50.160
aligned uh for me the scrappiness is kind of like&nbsp;
a runner's high like it's like oh hey we figured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.120
it out you know we were able to do it despite&nbsp;
the you know the setback or the challenge so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:02.960
um i always say like be be open to being scrappy&nbsp;
because i think it's a good skill set have you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.640
always been like that with other things where&nbsp;
you kind of you like problem solving in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.520
some capacity like if stuff's too easy it's&nbsp;
almost boring yeah it's funny like i feel like if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:19.200
something is too easy you're right it is a little&nbsp;
bit boring and i think like a lot of times there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:25.920
a perception right that things have to come&nbsp;
easy or things you know if you're doing it right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.480
if you're successful then you know you're you're&nbsp;
sipping you know drinks at the beach and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.680
not thinking about things and you know and that's&nbsp;
fine for like i don't know like a week a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.960
maybe but the rest of the year like i really&nbsp;
do enjoy a challenge like i think that's why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:49.040
um i enjoy filmmaking so much is because you know&nbsp;
trying to to frame you know a tiny portion of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.920
world in the camera and this little rectangle like&nbsp;
that's it and that's the challenge but it's funny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:04:01.440
like it sometimes is so all-encompassing like a&nbsp;
lot of times i will like have to stop myself and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.440
be like wow what i'm doing is kind of ridiculous&nbsp;
like you know we're all trying to pull this weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.160
shot off and you have to be able to almost&nbsp;
like laugh at yourself and be like this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:18.240
you know a little silly but at the end of the day&nbsp;
um it matters and it's you know the the little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.520
tool you take to tell the larger story um and&nbsp;
i think it's it's like being able to zoom out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.920
in that fashion that i think uh is invaluable just&nbsp;
to kind of put you know broader context into what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:35.680
you're attempting to do as a storyteller what&nbsp;
did film school not teach you that maybe there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.160
would be no class on it you just had to find&nbsp;
out on your own yeah there's no curriculum that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:47.840
could have showed you that yeah um i think film&nbsp;
school you know it didn't always teach me about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:56.160
the way the the hardship hits you when you&nbsp;
get out um i knew it would be difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.480
to you know get funding and i knew it would&nbsp;
be difficult uh you know if i went and did the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.640
film festival circuit because you know there's&nbsp;
certainly a lot of rejection there's certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:10.000
a lot of different things that happen there but&nbsp;
i think a lot of times they don't prepare you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.840
for the way to deal with it and that's you know&nbsp;
no slide against a film school that's not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:20.320
their thing that almost borders into like mental&nbsp;
health but i think there is some of that in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:26.160
film industry that maybe deserves to be talked&nbsp;
about um it's that it's okay to get rejected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.760
it's okay to make a film and have a couple&nbsp;
hundred views on youtube and call it a day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:39.040
um i think one of the things i've learned is&nbsp;
that you really can't get caught into your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.400
own work so much as an artist because uh you know&nbsp;
the goal like i mentioned earlier for me at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.600
is to just create a body of work and keep things&nbsp;
moving forward at all times and kind of continuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:56.800
a forward momentum and i feel like a lot of times&nbsp;
if you get too analytical with yourself you stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:02.640
moving forward and you stay really stagnant um and&nbsp;
it's it's challenging because you know these films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.840
a lot of times they're very personal stories&nbsp;
and they're things that we truly you know put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:13.040
our all into and so i think at the end of the day&nbsp;
it's certainly understandable that we care a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:18.400
